{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=Bay of Plenty}}
'''Rotorua''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It was first established in 1919, and has existed continuously since 1954. The current MP for Rotorua is [[Todd McClay]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]],<ref>[http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/3/a/2/50MP169901-McClay-Todd.htm Profile of Todd McClay] on New Zealand Parliament website.</ref> who won the electorate in the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]] from incumbent [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] MP [[Steve Chadwick]].

==Population centres==
In the 1918 electoral redistribution, the [[North Island]] gained a further three electorates from the [[South Island]] due to faster population growth. Only two existing electorates were unaltered, five electorates were abolished, two former electorate were re-established, and three electorates, including Rotorua, were created for the first time.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=75–80}}

The initial electorate, which was formed through the 1918 electoral redistribution, had a long coastline along the [[Bay of Plenty]], and incorporated, beside Rotorua, the towns and villages of [[Whakatāne]], [[Taupō]], [[Tokoroa]], [[Putāruru]], [[Mangakino]], [[Edgecumbe]], [[Tāneatua]], and [[Murupara]].{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=78f}} In the 1922 electoral redistribution, the electorate lost some area to the {{NZ electorate link|Bay of Plenty}} electorate, and a larger area to the {{NZ electorate link|Waikato}} electorate.{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=82f}} The 1927 electoral redistribution saw Rotorua become landlocked, with the {{NZ electorate link|Tauranga}} electorate taking the coastline including Tāneatua and Edgecumbe, and Whakatāne going to the Bay of Plenty electorate. The electorate moved south and took in [[Lake Taupō]], with [[Tūrangi]] just beyond the southern boundary located in the {{NZ electorate link|Waimarino}} electorate. The electorate also grew in the north-west, gaining the town of [[Matamata]].{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=86f}}

In the 1937 electoral redistribution, the electorate shifted further south again. Matamata was lost again, and the peaks of [[Mount Tongariro|Tongariro]], [[Mount Ngauruhoe|Ngauruhoe]], and [[Mount Ruapehu|Ruapehu]] now formed the boundary to the Waimarino electorate.{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=90f}} The 1946 electoral redistribution saw the Rotorua electorate abolished, with the Bay of Plenty electorate moving west and incorporating the town of Rotorua, most of the southern area going to the Waimarino electorate including the town of Taupō, and some area in the north-west going to the Waikato electorate including Tokoroa.{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=94f}}

The [[First Labour Government of New Zealand|First Labour Government]] was defeated in the {{NZ election link|1949}} and the incoming [[First National Government of New Zealand|National Government]] changed the Electoral Act, with the electoral quota once again based on total population as opposed to qualified electors, and the tolerance was increased to 7.5% of the electoral quota. There was no adjustments in the number of electorates between the [[South Island|South]] and [[North Island]]s, but the law changes resulted in boundary adjustments to almost every electorate through the 1952 electoral redistribution; only five electorates were unaltered.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=99f}} Five electorates were reconstituted (including Rotorua) and one was newly created, and a corresponding six electorates were abolished; all of these in the North Island.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=95–100}} These changes took effect with the {{NZ election link|1954}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=99}} The electorate was again landlocked and much smaller than prior to its abolition. Significant settlements included Rotorua, Tokoroa, Taupō, and Mangakino, with Lake Taupō forming the southern boundary.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=90, 98}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Rotorua.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The current Rotorua electorate is positioned in the [[Bay of Plenty]] region in the central North Island. It is dominated by the town of [[Rotorua]], and also contains the Eastern Bay of Plenty towns of [[Kawerau]], [[Murupara]] and [[Galatea, New Zealand|Galatea]], the last two of which are located on the outskirts of [[Te Urewera National Park]]. In 2008, its boundaries were extended to the geographical bay, with the addition of coastline stretching from a cluster of rural towns including Pukehina and [[Maketu]] to the outskirts of [[Te Puke]]. In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate expanded northward to the outskirts of [[Tauranga]] with the [[Tauranga Eastern Link]] forming part of the northern boundary. Initial proposals saw the rural towns of the [[Kaimai Range]] added to the electorate, though this was reverted after public consultation.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=21 September 2025 |page=20 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

== Demographics ==
Over forty per cent of the population of Rotorua is under the age of thirty, much of this because 37% of the electorate's residents are [[Māori people|Māori]], who are on the whole younger than the national average (22.7 years old versus a national average of 35.9).<ref>[http://www.tpk.govt.nz/en/in-print/kokiri/kokiri-01-2007/average-maori--who-is-she/ “Average” Māori – who is she? Te Puni Kōkiri: Kōkiri 1, 2007]</ref> There are also fewer voters earning over $30,000 per year, with the majority of workers coming from working class and semi-skilled professionals. Rotorua also has more unemployed people (6.5%) than most electorates, being ranked 52nd in the nation.

The [[country quota]] applied until 1945 and the Rotorua electorate was initially classed as fully rural. Based on the [[1926 New Zealand census]], the 1927 Electoral Redistribution determined that 24% of the electorate's population was urban. Based on the 1936 census, the 1937 Electoral Redistribution determined that 36% of the electorate's population was urban.{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=79–95}}

== History ==
An electorate based around Rotorua has been a part of the New Zealand electoral landscape since the {{NZ election link|1919}}, with a gap from {{NZ election link year|1946}} to 1954. Previously the town of Rotorua was in the {{NZ electorate link|East Coast}} electorate (from {{NZ election link year|1871}}), then the East Coast electorate again (from {{NZ election link year|1890}}), then the {{NZ electorate link|Bay of Plenty}} electorate (from {{NZ election link year|1893}}), and then (just) in the Tauranga electorate again (from {{NZ election link year|1911}} to 1919).{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=38–75}}

William Henry Wackrow was nominated in March 1922 as the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|opposition]] candidate for that [[1922 New Zealand general election|year's election]].<ref>{{cite news | url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AS19220304.2.37 | title=The General Election | work=[[Auckland Star]] | date=4 March 1922 | access-date=17 December 2014 | volume=LIII | issue=53 | page=6}}</ref> Wackrow withdrew in November<ref>{{cite news | url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP19221109.2.127 | title=The Election Campaign | work=[[The Press]] | date=9 November 1922 | access-date=17 December 2014 | volume=LVIII | issue=17607 | page=14}}</ref> and was replaced by [[Cecil Clinkard]], who lost against the incumbent, [[Frank Hockly]] of the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]].<ref name="1922 election" />

[[Geoffrey Sim]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] won the {{NZ election link|1943}}. When the Rotorua electorate was abolished for the {{NZ election link|1946}}, Sim successfully stood in Waikato electorate instead.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=234}}

After the electorate was re-established through the 1952 Electoral Redistribution, [[Ray Boord]] of the Labour Party won the {{NZ election link|1954}}.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=184}} Boord served two parliamentary terms and was beaten by National's [[Harry Lapwood]] in the {{NZ election link|1960}}.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=184, 211}} Lapwood served for six parliamentary terms and retired in 1978.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=211}}

Lapwood was succeeded by his party colleague [[Paul East]] in the {{NZ election link|1978}}. East also served six parliamentary terms until 1996. With the advent of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|Mixed Member Proportional]] (MMP) voting in {{NZ election link year|1996}}, the Rotorua electorate was greatly expanded to include areas previously part of the {{NZ electorate link|Eastern Bay of Plenty}} and {{NZ electorate link|Tarawera}} electorates. Both Tarawera and Rotorua were safe National Party electorates, and in the ensuing battle for the nomination, the two incumbents, East and [[Max Bradford]], faced off for a Rotorua nomination eventually secured by Bradford, with East securing a high [[Party lists in the 1996 New Zealand general election#National Party|list position]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Part III – Party Lists of Successful Registered Parties |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/3.3%20Party%20Lists%20of%20Successful%20Registered%20Parties.pdf |publisher=Electoral Commission |access-date=14 June 2013 }}</ref>

Bradford won the 1996 election with a nearly 6,000 votes margin.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/6.1%20Rotorua%2046.pdf |title=Electorate Candidate and Party Votes Recorded at Each Polling Place – Rotorua, 1996 |access-date=16 November 2013 }}</ref> Despite both electorates being reasonably loyal to the National Party, Bradford's tenure as MP for Rotorua was just three years, before being ousted by Labour MP [[Steve Chadwick]] in the {{NZ election link|1999}}. Chadwick's initial majority of 4,978 votes blew out to over 7,500 in [[2002 New Zealand general election|2002]] before it was reined in to just 662 in [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005]], as the National Party consolidated the centre-right vote, with its biggest gains being in the provincial North Island. In 2005, Chadwick's party was less popular than their candidate, coming 1,645 votes behind National.

In 2008 Chadwick was defeated by National candidate [[Todd McClay]] who won the electorate with a majority of 5,067 votes. In the 2011 election McClay again returned as the member for Rotorua, increasing his majority to 7,357 votes. In 2014, McClay was elected as MP for a third term beating television personality [[Tāmati Coffey]] by a similar majority to that in the previous election.

Rotorua is also an electorate where the [[New Zealand First]] party does well, with its biggest appeal among provincial New Zealanders, and as results in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]] indicate, Māori: in the three most recent elections<!-- as of 2008 election -->, New Zealand First has polled around three per cent higher in Rotorua than it did in the rest of New Zealand.

